import "core/list.ct"


module(Data)
  # # Files
    A [FileOrDir] is a tree of files: a [File], which is a name and what is written in it, or a
    [Dir], which is a name and the entries beneath it. It is the shape a directory has, written as a
    value, so a program can be given one to read or can produce one to be written.
    It is declared here rather than beside whatever wants it because more than one thing wants it. A
    build result is a tree of files, and a directory read from disk is a tree of files, and neither
    is the other. A type declared inside one of them would have to be reached through it by the
    other, which says that the second use is a kind of the first when it is not.
  class(FileOrDir, [File(name -> String, contents -> String), Dir(name -> String, entries -> /Data/List[$T -> FileOrDir])])
    # A [File] carries its writing itself, rather than the path it was read from or is to be written
      to. A tree says where each thing is by where it is in the tree, so a name is the one part of a
      path a file holds, and the rest of it is the names of the directories above it. This is what
      lets the same tree be written somewhere other than where it was read from, which is the whole
      of what an overlay does.
      [contents] is a [String] because that is what the language has. A file which is not text is
      not representable here, and will want a type of its own rather than this one widened to hold
      bytes it cannot say anything about.

  # ## Read by destructuring
    Nothing uses this yet, but it does work. A [Dir] holding both a [File] and a [Dir] can be
    written and can be walked, by arms matching the two constructors — which is how every other
    recursive type here is read, [ConsList] and [BinTree] alike.
    What cannot be written is `d.entries`. A field is read by destructuring it, not through an
    accessor named after it, so `count(f= Dir(name, entries))` reaches the entries and `d.entries`
    reaches nothing whether the list under it is mixed or not.
    This section used to say the type was unbuildable, because a list literal was thought to take
    its element type from its first entry. It does not: `[Some(value= 1), Nothing]` typechecks, and
    so does a [Dir] holding a [File] beside a [Dir]. The accessor was what failed, and the reason
    given for it was wrong.
    A build result is still the flat [/Catln/BuildFile] list in @core/result.ct@, which is a
    separate choice: a result is a list of files so that every entry of it is the same kind of
    thing, and the tree is said by the @/@ in each path.
  # ## Naming
    The name of an entry is the one it has under its parent, not a path: a [Dir] named `notes`
    holding a [File] named `a.md` is the file `notes/a.md`. Nothing here checks that a name is one a
    filesystem accepts, because a tree is a value and may be built long before anything writes it —
    whoever writes it is who has to refuse a name it cannot write, and does.
